  "notes": "The folk-facing end of the lane, and the entry names the one parameter that separates her from the rest of it: \"major-mode is her default — G, F, D, C, B and A major dominate\", giving \"diatonic major harmony with add9 and sus voicings from a strummed guitar rather than jazz minor extensions\". So this is the only model in the batch whose triad weight is higher than its ninth weight, and `susSubstitutionProb` is raised for the sus shapes the research names. The three progression families are the three the entry writes out. The band is the sourced 54–106 straight on 2&4. Drums encode the stated absence: \"soft, brushed or programmed-quiet kit with the snare well back in the mix, tambourine or shaker carrying the 8ths, and frequent full drop-out of the kit for whole verses\" — hence the lowest kick density and hat fill density here, a non-continuous hat, quiet velocity tiers, and a percussion layer that is louder than the kit rather than quieter. Melody is \"mid-register, syllabic, low-melisma, hymn-like, and often doubled by the guitar line\", which is the stepwise interval spread, the high chord-tone bias and an `octave_echo` counter. Bass is \"root-and-fifth, short phrases, wide gaps\", so it mirrors the kick at a very high root probability with a low anticipation. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum number, the velocity tiers and all melody/bass numbers — \"soft\", \"well back\", \"short phrases\" and \"wide gaps\" are directions, not figures. ⚠ The research marks its 119–188 readings as doubled [LOW-CONF]; the folded band is what is encoded. ⚠ The id drops the period per the dataset's convention for punctuated names; \"india arie\" is carried as an alias.",
